                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="1214-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_o.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Omnidirectional" title="Omnidirectional" ><img id="1214-1" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_c.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Cardioid" title="Cardioid" ><img id="1214-2" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_8.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Bidirectional" title="Bidirectional" ></span>Behringer B-2 Pro</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Multi-Pattern Condenser Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The Behringer B-2 Pro is a multipattern large diaphragm FET condenser. It appears to be identical internally to the NT2S from 797 Audio.<br /><div class="shaft"><a rel="shadowbox" href="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images//mic_extras/behringer/NT2S-B2Pro1.jpg"><img src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/mic_extras/behringer/NT2S-B2Pro1-sm.jpg" class="thumb shaft" alt="B-2 Pro PCB" width="200" /></a><br /><a rel="shadowbox" href="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images//mic_extras/behringer/NT2S-B2Pro2.jpg"><img src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/mic_extras/behringer/NT2S-B2Pro2-sm.jpg" class="thumb shaft" alt="B-2 Pro PCB" width="200" /></a></div></p><p>The circuit board is built with through-hole components, facilitating DIY mods and upgrades.</p><p>The mic uses a dual-diaphragm capsule that is shockmounted within the headbasket. A switch on the mic body allows selection of three polar patterns: Cardioid, Omni, or Figure-8.</p><p>A -10dB attenuation and 125Hz low cut are available via switches on rear of the mic body.</p><p>Included accessories for the B-2 Pro: aluminum case, foam wind filter, shock mount.<p>Permalink: <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/Behringer/B-2Pro">Behringer B-2Pro</a></p><p>The mic was released in 2002.</p>
